WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
The first film found the ceiling.

2
00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:08.280
In healthy people running or cycling at sea level, oxygen delivery mainly sets it.

3
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.360
But no one races for long at the ceiling.

4
00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.600
As the pace rises, blood lactate climbs faster.

5
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.760
Is the muscle running out of oxygen?

6
00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:26.163
A 2008 review names three things behind endurance.

7
00:00:27.443 --> 00:00:28.483
The ceiling itself.

8
00:00:29.783 --> 00:00:32.043
The fraction of it a person can sustain.

9
00:00:32.983 --> 00:00:36.883
And economy, the oxygen cost of moving at a given speed.

10
00:00:38.083 --> 00:00:40.983
That fraction is related to the lactate threshold.

11
00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:44.933
To see that threshold, start with lactate.

12
00:00:46.113 --> 00:00:47.453
It is a fuel, not waste.

13
00:00:48.733 --> 00:00:54.073
Some pyruvate from glycolysis becomes lactate and leaves the fibres that make it.

14
00:00:55.213 --> 00:00:58.993
Other fibres and the heart burn it, and the liver makes glucose from it.

15
00:00:59.933 --> 00:01:01.453
How much fuel is that?

16
00:01:02.593 --> 00:01:08.093
In one tracer study of 12 trained and untrained men, blood lactate supplied an

17
00:01:08.093 --> 00:01:10.653
estimated third of carbohydrate energy.

18
00:01:11.853 --> 00:01:14.693
That was over 60 minutes at the lactate threshold.

19
00:01:15.883 --> 00:01:18.163
So the lactate in blood is a balance.

20
00:01:19.403 --> 00:01:20.603
Here is the key idea.

21
00:01:22.003 --> 00:01:26.943
Blood lactate is set by the balance of appearance and clearance: how fast it enters,

22
00:01:27.283 --> 00:01:28.903
against how well it is removed.

23
00:01:30.203 --> 00:01:32.503
As work rises, appearance keeps rising.

24
00:01:33.633 --> 00:01:35.933
Now raise the work rate step by step.

25
00:01:37.153 --> 00:01:38.873
Appearance climbs with every step.

26
00:01:40.093 --> 00:01:43.333
Suppose clearance has already reached its peak and holds there.

27
00:01:44.613 --> 00:01:45.933
What does blood lactate do?

28
00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:56.430
It climbs faster, because appearance outpaces clearance more with every step.

29
00:01:57.730 --> 00:02:01.430
In one study of trained men, clearance did peak below the threshold.

30
00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:06.330
That fits the faster climb, but does not prove it causes the bend.

31
00:02:07.433 --> 00:02:12.573
That bend marks the lactate threshold: the workload above which blood lactate

32
00:02:12.573 --> 00:02:14.033
accumulation speeds up.

33
00:02:15.173 --> 00:02:17.013
It has no universal value.

34
00:02:18.233 --> 00:02:22.173
It is not a fixed 4 mmol per L, or a fixed share of the ceiling.

35
00:02:23.317 --> 00:02:25.637
Nor is it where oxygen runs out.

36
00:02:26.937 --> 00:02:30.897
Above the threshold, muscle keeps consuming oxygen and burning fuel.

37
00:02:32.317 --> 00:02:34.177
Lactate is still cleared and still burned.

38
00:02:35.477 --> 00:02:36.737
Only the balance has tipped.

39
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.940
The same study compared trained and untrained men.

40
00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.120
At each group's own threshold, clearance was 34 percent higher in the trained men.

41
00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:51.120
Comparing groups shows an association, not cause.

42
00:02:52.233 --> 00:02:56.313
So the bend in the curve is a balance tipping, not oxygen failing.

43
00:02:57.813 --> 00:03:01.793
Lactate keeps flowing from the fibres that make it to the tissues that use it.

44
00:03:03.073 --> 00:03:06.473
Above the threshold, it simply arrives faster than it is cleared.
